# Priority Roadmap Toward a General Theory of Sustainability

## Governing principle

Prioritize work that increases unification, formal coherence, nontrivial explanatory power, and empirical contact. Do not spend equal effort repairing every historical draft. Where a later source supplies a stronger canonical result, preserve the earlier source but integrate only its unique content.

## Priority 1 — Canonicalize the formal spine

### Objective

Establish one official specification, architecture tuple, notation system, policy/disturbance quantifier convention, constraint registry, and theorem-source hierarchy.

### Why first

Without canonicalization, every later theorem or module risks entering a competing formalism. This is the highest-leverage step for a general theory.

### Required work

- unify the master architecture with Article 002;
- resolve notation collisions across Articles 001–007;
- select canonical sources for duplicated results;
- map Article 006/007 unique institutional content into the canonical architecture;
- mark superseded formulations without deleting them.

### Key actions

`X-A001-A002-01`, `X-A001-A002-11`, `B-A002-01`, `B-A002-02`, `B-A002-03`, `V-A002-03`, `V-A002-06`, `V-A006-14`, `V-A007-10`.

### Deliverable

A canonical formal-specification document and notation/theorem-source registry from which the flagship is rebuilt.

