## Priority 3 — Develop Operator II as a genuine transformation theory

### Objective

Move beyond a placeholder architecture graph to a formal theory of architecture-changing sustainability.

### Why third

Transformation is one of the project’s most distinctive claims and the main element not already supplied by classical viability theory.

### Required work

- define the hybrid architecture state space and meta-policy class;
- formalize cross-architecture translation of stocks, obligations, beliefs, identities, and liabilities;
- define admissible transition relaxations and non-relaxable constraints;
- connect emergency recovery inside an architecture to transformation between architectures;
- characterize transformability, impossibility within a registered architecture class, and option loss;
- construct at least one complete toy theorem/example.

### Deliverable

A restricted but nontrivial transformation theorem or exact worked model, not only a definition.

---

## Priority 4 — Prove compositional sustainability for restricted classes

### Objective

Show when subsystem certificates compose and when coupling destroys or creates viability.

### Why fourth

A theory cannot be general across ecological, economic, and social systems if it lacks a defensible composition rule.

### Required work

- verify Article 001’s restricted composition and coupling results;
- integrate Article 002’s typed contracts;
- include shared-control feasibility, confidence, timing, disturbance correlation, and boundary interfaces;
- connect patch coupling, cascade networks, and commons games;
- retain counterexamples showing why local viability is insufficient.

### Key actions

`B-A001-08`, `I-A001-06`, `X-A001-A002-06`, `V-A001-06`, plus the commons-game checks `V-A001-07`, `V-A001-09`, and `V-A001-10`.

### Deliverable

At least one sound compositional theorem, one destruction counterexample, one rescue example, and a clearly bounded general conjecture.

---

## Priority 5 — Ground the architecture in one complete domain case

### Objective

Take one domain from boundary definition through data, model comparison, viability/recovery analysis, and prospective falsification.

### Why fifth

One complete application is more valuable than several uncalibrated module templates. It tests whether the architecture produces new diagnoses or decisions.

### Candidate domains

- **Groundwater:** strongest test of partial observation, authority, implementation, delayed recovery, and physical–institutional governability.
- **Phosphorus/catchment:** strongest test of material accounting, trade displacement, multi-scale interfaces, substitution, and ecological receiving-water safety.

### Selection rule

Choose the case with the best presently available boundary, data, thresholds, institutional records, and held-out validation opportunity. Do not develop both simultaneously unless resources justify it.

### Required work

- apply Article 007’s corrected module-admission standard;
- resolve the selected domain’s critical verification queue;
- preregister H0/H1/H2 comparator models;
- estimate uncertainty and interface flows;
- compute or approximate a kernel, capture basin, obstruction, or decision margin;
- compare with simpler models.

### Deliverable

One auditable end-to-end case capable of surprising or falsifying the architecture.

---

## Priority 6 — Rebuild institutional and distributive operationalization

### Objective

Develop valid measurable institutional and distributional modules without reusing the flawed scalar indices in Articles 008–009.

### Why later

These dimensions are essential, but the current drafts require redesign rather than incremental repair. Building them before the formal spine and one empirical case would risk premature metric construction.

### Institutional direction

Use a vector of observation, latency, authority, implementation, enforcement, mandate load, compliance, learning, and time-to-exit margins. Treat monetary intensity only as a possible covariate.

### Distributive direction

Use typed normalized component margins, exact nonsmooth conjunction or conservative smooth inner certificates, validated indicators, correct pooled distributions, and explicit intergenerational transitions.

### Deliverable

Validated modules or a distinct empirical/methods paper only if independent results merit publication.

---

## Priority 7 — Nonlinear transitions and advanced dynamics

### Objective

Develop sampled-versus-delay institutional feedback, bifurcation persistence, and safety-relevant reachable-tube analysis.

### Why later

The mechanism is important but depends on the canonical policy, information, phase-space, and safety architecture. The current variant archive is not yet reproducible.

### Required work

- register equations, parameters, code, and outputs;
- separate H1/H2/H3 feedback signs;
- derive sampled-to-delay bridges where appropriate;
- validate periodic branches and multipliers;
- measure transient reachable-tube safety rather than bifurcation existence alone;
- merge the duplicate persistence conjectures.

---

## Priority 8 — Novelty, literature, and final publication partition

### Objective

After the canonical theorems and one application are clear, establish exact novelty and choose the minimum merited publication set.

### Required work

- complete `LIT-GLOBAL-01`;
- compare theorem-by-theorem with viability, partial observation, hybrid safety, SES, ecological economics, commons, and contract literature;
- distinguish new theorem, synthesis, interface, counterexample, and empirical result;
- decide between one flagship plus supplement and a two-paper minimum;
- avoid separate papers for superseded or module-only drafts.

## Work explicitly deprioritized

- repairing every line of Articles 006–007 when a stronger canonical source already exists;
- integrating Article 008’s solvency index or Article 009’s distributive theorem in current form;
- developing multiple empirical modules in parallel without one end-to-end case;
- expanding the conceptual architecture before current proof obligations are discharged;
- deciding final paper count before source ingestion and canonicalization are sufficiently complete.
